"""Owner-facing prognosis copy: useful clinical orientation without mini literature reviews."""

DOG = {
'lymphoma': 'Lymphoma is usually treatable but not usually cured. Many dogs with common high-grade B-cell lymphoma respond well to chemotherapy and can have many good-quality months; T-cell lymphoma and some less common forms may behave more aggressively. The most useful prognosis comes from the lymphoma type, grade, location, overall health and response to treatment.',
'mast cell tumor': 'The outlook for mast cell tumors ranges from excellent to serious. A small low-grade tumor that can be completely removed may be cured with surgery, while high-grade, recurrent, node-positive or metastatic disease needs a more guarded outlook and often additional treatment. Grade, mitotic activity, margins and lymph-node status matter more than one average survival number.',
'soft tissue sarcoma': 'Many low- and intermediate-grade soft tissue sarcomas can be controlled for a long time when the first local treatment is well planned. Higher grade, large size, recurrence, difficult location and metastatic spread worsen the outlook. For an individual dog, grade and the ability to achieve durable local control are usually more useful than a single survival statistic.',
'hemangiosarcoma': 'Visceral hemangiosarcoma is an aggressive cancer with a substantial risk of internal bleeding and metastatic spread. Surgery can address a bleeding or removable primary tumor, and chemotherapy may extend control for some dogs, but recurrence or spread is common. Superficial skin hemangiosarcoma can behave much less aggressively and should not be given the same prognosis as splenic or cardiac disease.',
'osteosarcoma': 'Appendicular osteosarcoma is aggressive because microscopic spread is often present even when chest imaging looks clear. Treating the painful primary bone tumor can restore comfort, and chemotherapy can delay metastatic progression for many dogs. Visible metastasis, tumor location and some laboratory and pathology features can change the outlook substantially.',
'oral melanoma': 'Canine oral melanoma can be both locally invasive and prone to metastatic spread. Earlier, smaller tumors generally have a better outlook than large tumors or disease that has reached lymph nodes or lungs. Stage and the ability to control the primary tumor are more useful for an individual dog than one overall melanoma survival number.',
'melanoma': 'Melanoma does not have one prognosis. Many cutaneous melanomas are relatively well behaved, while oral, mucosal and some digital melanomas can be much more aggressive. The primary site, pathology and whether disease has spread determine what the diagnosis means for an individual dog.',
'oral squamous cell carcinoma': 'Canine oral squamous cell carcinoma is often primarily a local-control problem, and some dogs with tumors that can be completely removed can do well for a long time. Tumor site, size, bone invasion, lymph-node involvement and the ability to obtain clean local control are the major factors that shape prognosis.',
'squamous cell carcinoma': 'The outlook for squamous cell carcinoma depends heavily on where it starts. A small skin lesion may be curable with local treatment, while tumors in some oral, nasal or other sites can be much more invasive. Site, depth, stage and the ability to obtain local control are more useful than a generic SCC survival number.',
'urothelial carcinoma': 'Urothelial carcinoma is usually managed rather than cured, but many dogs can have meaningful periods of good quality of life with treatment. Tumor location matters greatly because disease near the bladder neck, urethra or prostate can threaten urine flow. Obstruction, metastatic spread and response to treatment are major factors in the outlook.',
'hepatocellular carcinoma': 'A solitary liver hepatocellular carcinoma that can be completely removed can have a very favorable outlook, and some dogs live for years after surgery. Multifocal, diffuse, invasive or metastatic liver cancer is a very different situation. The first prognostic question is therefore whether the tumor is truly solitary and safely resectable.',
'mammary carcinoma': 'Canine mammary carcinoma ranges from relatively low-risk localized disease to aggressive metastatic cancer. Smaller tumors that are completely removed and lack high-risk pathology features generally have a better outlook. Tumor size, grade, lymphovascular invasion, lymph-node involvement and distant spread are the most useful prognostic pieces.',
'thyroid carcinoma': 'Many dogs with localized thyroid carcinoma can have long periods of good quality of life, especially when the tumor is mobile and can be controlled locally. Fixed invasive tumors, major-vessel involvement and metastatic disease make treatment more difficult. Resectability and stage are therefore central to prognosis.',
'prostate cancer': 'Canine prostate cancer is usually aggressive and can cause major local problems as well as metastatic spread. The outlook depends on whether urine flow can be maintained, how extensive the disease is and whether meaningful local and systemic treatment is possible. Selected dogs who can receive effective local treatment may do substantially better than dogs with advanced obstructive or metastatic disease.',
'primary lung tumor': 'A solitary primary lung tumor that can be completely removed can have a meaningful period of control and sometimes a long survival. Multiple lung lesions, pleural disease, aggressive pathology or spread to chest lymph nodes worsen the outlook. Confirming that the lesion is a primary lung tumor rather than metastasis from another cancer is also important.',
'glioma': 'Glioma is a serious brain tumor, but treatment can provide meaningful control for some dogs. The outlook depends on tumor location, neurologic condition, control of seizures or brain swelling and which local treatment is feasible. Published outcomes vary widely, so an individual treatment plan is more useful than a broad survival range.',
'meningioma': 'Many canine meningiomas can be controlled for a meaningful period with surgery, radiation or both, depending on location. Tumors that are accessible and causing reversible compression may have a better outlook than lesions in difficult locations or dogs with severe neurologic compromise. MRI findings and treatment options matter more than one average survival figure.',
'nasal tumor': 'Canine nasal tumors are usually locally invasive, and local progression is often the main threat. Radiation can provide meaningful control for many dogs, but the outlook varies with tumor type and how far disease extends within the nasal cavity and toward the brain. More extensive local invasion generally carries a more guarded prognosis.',
'leukemia': 'Leukemia does not have one prognosis. Acute leukemia can progress rapidly and is often difficult to control, while some chronic leukemias can be managed for much longer periods. Cell lineage, whether disease is acute or chronic, blood counts and response to treatment are essential before giving an individual outlook.',
'multiple myeloma': 'Multiple myeloma is a systemic cancer, but many dogs respond well to treatment and can have a meaningful period of good quality of life. Kidney injury, high calcium, severe anemia, abnormal blood proteins and bone disease can make the situation more serious. Response to treatment is one of the most important indicators over time.',
'chemodectoma': 'Many heart-base tumors grow relatively slowly, and some dogs can live well for a substantial time with monitoring or treatment. Prognosis depends less on the name alone than on whether the tumor is growing, compressing nearby structures, causing fluid around the heart or chest, producing arrhythmias or spreading elsewhere.',
}

CAT = {
'lymphoma': 'Feline lymphoma has no single prognosis because its forms behave very differently. Some low-grade intestinal lymphomas can be controlled for years, while high-grade, renal, mediastinal and other forms may require more intensive treatment and can have a shorter course. Anatomic form, grade, FeLV status when relevant and response to treatment are more useful than one overall survival number.',
'mast cell tumor': 'The outlook for feline mast cell tumors depends strongly on where the disease is located. A solitary skin mast cell tumor can often be cured with surgery, and splenic disease can sometimes have a favorable course after splenectomy. Intestinal, disseminated or recurrent disease is more concerning. Canine mast cell tumor grading and survival expectations should not be applied directly to cats.',
'soft tissue sarcoma': 'Many feline soft tissue sarcomas are primarily a local-control problem, but injection-site sarcomas can be especially infiltrative and prone to local recurrence. The best outlook comes when the first definitive treatment is planned widely enough to control microscopic extension. Grade, location, recurrence and metastatic spread also matter.',
'hemangiosarcoma': 'Feline hemangiosarcoma is uncommon, so there is not enough evidence for a dependable single survival estimate. Superficial skin disease can behave differently from visceral disease, which may bleed and spread. Tumor location, whether complete surgery is possible and whether metastatic disease is present are the most useful prognostic factors.',
'osteosarcoma': 'Feline osteosarcoma often behaves less aggressively than the typical canine form, especially in a limb, but metastatic spread still occurs and should not be dismissed. Cats with a resectable tumor and no visible spread generally have a better outlook than cats with axial, unresectable or metastatic disease. Site, stage and local control matter most.',
'oral melanoma': 'Feline oral melanoma is rare and appears capable of aggressive local growth and metastatic spread, but the evidence is too limited for a reliable feline survival estimate. Diagnostic certainty, tumor size and location, local invasion and whether disease has spread are more useful for discussing an individual cat.',
'melanoma': 'Melanoma is uncommon in cats and its behavior depends strongly on where it starts. Ocular, oral and cutaneous melanomas can have very different courses. Because feline data are limited, prognosis should be based on the primary site, pathology and stage rather than on canine melanoma statistics.',
'oral squamous cell carcinoma': 'Feline oral squamous cell carcinoma is usually a difficult, locally aggressive cancer. The outlook is often guarded because many tumors cannot be completely removed by the time they are found. Exact location, jaw or tongue involvement, bone invasion, ability to eat and whether meaningful local control is possible are central to prognosis.',
'squamous cell carcinoma': 'Feline SCC has very different outlooks depending on location. Small sun-associated lesions of the ears, eyelids or nasal planum can often be controlled well when treated early, while oral SCC is much more aggressive. Site, depth, local invasion and ability to obtain complete local control matter more than a generic SCC statistic.',
'urothelial carcinoma': 'Feline urothelial carcinoma is uncommon, so reliable survival estimates are limited. The immediate outlook depends heavily on tumor location, whether urine flow is threatened, kidney function and whether disease has spread. A favorably located tumor that can be controlled locally is a different situation from obstructive or metastatic disease.',
'hepatocellular carcinoma': 'A solitary liver hepatocellular carcinoma that can be completely removed may have a favorable outlook in a cat. Multifocal, diffuse, invasive or metastatic disease is much harder to control. Because feline data are limited, resectability, surgical anatomy and stage are more useful than quoting a small published series.',
'mammary carcinoma': 'Feline mammary carcinoma is usually biologically aggressive, but the outlook still varies substantially between cats. Smaller tumors found before lymph-node or distant spread generally have a better prognosis. Tumor size, grade, lymphovascular invasion, lymph-node status and metastatic stage are the major factors to discuss.',
'thyroid carcinoma': 'Feline thyroid carcinoma is rare, so there is no dependable survival number that applies to every cat. Some localized or iodine-responsive tumors can be controlled for a long time, while invasive or metastatic tumors are more difficult. Local invasion, spread, kidney function and whether the tumor is iodine-avid help determine the realistic options and outlook.',
'prostate cancer': 'Feline prostate cancer is exceptionally rare, and the available evidence is too sparse for a reliable survival estimate. Reported tumors can be locally aggressive and metastatic. For an individual cat, the most important questions are whether urination or defecation is compromised, how far the disease extends and what local control is realistically possible.',
'primary lung tumor': 'A solitary primary lung tumor that can be completely removed may have a meaningful period of control in a cat. Spread to chest lymph nodes, pleura or other organs, multiple lesions and aggressive pathology worsen the outlook. Because feline series are limited, stage and resectability are more useful than a single median survival.',
'glioma': 'Feline glioma is rare, and there is not enough feline evidence for a dependable survival estimate. The outlook depends on tumor location, neurologic condition, control of seizures or brain swelling and whether radiation, surgery or another local treatment is feasible. Canine survival figures should not be substituted for feline data.',
'meningioma': 'Feline meningioma is one of the more treatable primary brain tumors in cats, and selected cats can have long periods of good quality of life after successful local treatment. Tumor location, surgical accessibility, neurologic condition and completeness of local control determine the individual outlook.',
'nasal tumor': 'The outlook for a feline nasal tumor depends strongly on what the biopsy shows. Nasal lymphoma can be quite treatment-responsive, while carcinomas and other tumors follow different courses. Local extent, tumor type, ability to breathe comfortably and response to treatment matter more than one generic nasal-cancer survival number.',
'leukemia': 'Feline leukemia has no single prognosis. Acute leukemias can progress quickly and are often difficult to control, while some chronic leukemias can be managed for much longer. Cell lineage, acute versus chronic disease, blood counts, FeLV status when relevant and response to treatment are essential for an individual outlook.',
'multiple myeloma': 'Feline multiple myeloma is uncommon, so the evidence base is limited. Some cats respond to systemic treatment, but kidney injury, high calcium, anemia, abnormal blood proteins and bone lesions can make disease more serious. The individual response to therapy is especially important when judging prognosis over time.',
'chemodectoma': 'Chemodectoma is extremely rare in cats, so there is no reliable feline survival estimate. The practical outlook depends on whether the heart-base mass is growing or causing compression, fluid accumulation, arrhythmia or metastatic spread. Canine treatment outcomes should be treated only as indirect guidance.',
'histiocytic sarcoma': 'Feline histiocytic sarcoma is rare and can be aggressive. Localized disease that can be completely controlled is a different situation from disseminated disease involving several organs. Because feline outcome data are limited, extent of disease, local-control options and the cat’s clinical condition are more useful than borrowing canine survival statistics.',
}
